<blockquote>
Event Ideas: Officers and Committees<br />
Written by <a href="mailto:mayhem@conclaveofguilds.com">Mayhem</a><br />
<br />
In the last Event Ideas article we took a look at some things guilds can do
early on, even before there is a game to play in.  Since we're taking a
closer look at the organization of a guild this month, we'll just focus on
the organizational structure behind the scenes.  This should be of use to
those guilds trying to figure out how they want to set up the event process,
as well as those that may already have a system in place but wish to refine
it.<br />
<br />
There are a number of organizational structures which may be followed in
regards to the "Events Division" of the guild.  This is difficult to
represent in a text format, so bear with me as I lay it out for you.  I'll
break it down into various general types, from simple to complex.<br />
<br />
The simplest system is having the Guild Leader organize all events.  This
works best in small guilds, obviously, as the workload of the Guild Leader
increases as a guild grows.  It does offer "personal control" over things,
which can be advantageous in certain types of guilds.<br />
<br />
The next level of complexity is reached when an Events Officer is added.
This individual reports up the line, and handles the organization and
execution of the events.  This model of organization suits medium-sized
guilds, and is relatively uncomplicated.<br />
<br />
For slightly larger guilds (though still mid-sized) it can be beneficial to
add an Events Committee into the mix.  The size of this group depends on the
size of the guild and the desire of the guild members to participate in
various events.  Typically a group of two or three members led by the Events
Officer is sufficient.<br />
<br />
Large guilds with an eye on more than raiding may benefit from the addition
of a new high-level position.  This "Operations Officer" reports up the line
(typically to the Guild Leader or Assistant Guild Leader) and then has
overall responsibility for the officers beneath.  Down the chain would be
the Events Officer and the Raiding Officer, as well as the associated
committees.  This offers a distinct advantage in overall coordination in
large guilds.<br />
<br />
For the largest guilds, or those which hold the senior position in an
alliance, it may be beneficial to further complicate the structure.  In this
instance, multiple officers would be installed beneath the Events Officer,
each one with their own Committee.  These positions would be responsible for
a revolving set of tasks, depending on which events were upcoming.<br />
<br />
While it may cause some to shudder under the bureaucratic load, the more
complex structures have definite advantages.  When something falls through
in a small guild, there will likely be someone aware and able to catch the
slip before it becomes a problem.  If such happens in a large guild where
there is an uncomplicated structure in place, the chances of such mistakes
being a real problem increases.  At the very least, the Guild Leader may
find more difficulty in the regular rectification of such situations.<br />
<br />
The other major advantage of creating a structure with complexity is that it
becomes easier to keep the ratio of officers to members up.  In the first
structure, there are no officer positions created.  By the time we get down
to the fifth and final example, we find that for the Operations Section
(including events and raids) there are perhaps seven to nine, or even more
potential officer positions.  This can be a boon to those very large guilds
which have perhaps 150-200 members.  If you incorporate such a structure
into your charter and allow the appointments of certain officers to last a
temporary (say 3 to 4 month) period, this allows many individuals to
experience responsibility, without undermining key positions in the guild.
Officer positions throughout the guild may be rotated in this manner, though
it is best to leave the rotations at such a low level, thus preserving the
senior staff.<br />
<br />
Bear in mind that these structures can start out and be built up, but it is
important not to disrupt your original charter too greatly.  Thus be sure to
make provisions for future expansion when you write up the charter!  While a
guild doing something "unconstitutional" is not a great deal of problem, it
can cause needless internal friction.  Imagine if a guild which previously
had no Events Officer (all events were dealt with by the Guild Leader) adds
one.  Now imagine that the individual appointed to the position is someone
who used to play Diablo 2 with the Guild Leader.  Then all of the sudden
certain (probably jealous) guild members start saying that the only reason
the position was created was so that one of the Guild Leader's buddies could
be an officer.  Then imagine the annoying tedium of trying to explain the
situation.  Better to just avoid it, right?<br />
<br />
The process of event scheduling, planning and execution is going to differ
wildly depending on the structure you use as well as the individuals
involved.  It is very important to lay out certain procedures and then stick
to them.  Revising a process makes sense if you can make it function better,
of course, but don't attempt to go at things chaotically and expect to be as
successful as those who take a more ordered approach.  The phrase "like a
well-oiled machine" should describe the manner in which a successful guild
is run, and part of this is the way in which events are handled.<br />
<br />
In general, the more "layered" your structure, the more work is handled at
the bottom for any given issue.  For example, if you have the most complex
structure (number five) described above, the Operations Officer knows a bit
about everything going on with events and raids, but is not perhaps
intimately familiar with the debate going on below.  The things that reach
this level tend to be solid plans, ready to be "signed off on" and the final
details publicized.  Meanwhile the Events Officer below handles a bit more
of the details and coordinates the delegation of projects, handing out tasks
to the officers and committees at the lowest level.  The individuals "in the
trenches" are the most involved in planning and scheduling the particulars
of any event.  They  may have a few projects to plan at a time, and as they
finish, the work they have done is passed back up the line for final
approval.<br />
<br />
Now, when you compare this to the first structure where all planning is done
by the Guild Leader, or even by the second structure, where the Events
Officer handles these duties it seems absurd.  However, while one or two
people can handle planning events for a guild of a modest size, it is quite
a different story when the guild sponsors a newbie event once a week, an
arena contest once a month, a crafts fair and auction twice a month, a
recruiting junket twice a month, plus one more event each week which changes
depending on what the Events Division comes up with.  Don't even try to
consider adding a raiding schedule to that and trying to coordinate if you
don't have the staff to take on the burden, let alone trying to figure out
what to do with a guild that spans multiple (Americas and EMEA for example)
regions.<br />
<br />
Now, you may still be thinking I'm insane at this point.  Suggesting that a
group of four officers plus four members be tasked with handling events (and
all but one of them ONLY events) even within a large, active guild may seem
a bit daft to some people.  However if you are a guild that wants to make a
big impact, you need to put in the effort.  Unless the Guild Leader is
independently wealthy and doesn't need to work or study, there's no way that
they can handle the task alone and still be able to play the game a
reasonable amount.  Thus in order to be fair and not pile too much work on
one person, it's vital that the workload be distributed well.<br />
<br />
Once your structure is established, be sure to set up clear procedures for
handling the development and execution of events.  Event ideas should come
from within the Events Division as well as the general member body.
Submissions for event ideas should be taken in a standard format, be it a
forum, form, email, or simply a suggestion in game.  Whatever options your
guild supports, be sure the method(s) to be used is(are) clear to the
members.<br />
<br />
Ideas which have been submitted should be researched by the Events Division.
Things to take into considerations include the schedule of the guild, the
similarity of the planned event to those sponsored by other guilds, and the
viability of the idea.  For example, if another guild plans to hold a "naked
halfling race", it would be best if this was not duplicated.  As well, it
makes little sense to plan an event when the schedule of the guild is so
full of raids and previously planned events that members would need to be
logged in for an excessive period of time.  It is the responsibility of the
Events Division to ensure that when an event is planned it will be
successful.  Thus if an event were submitted that involved a "24 hour
marathon levelling contest" the logical conclusion would be that it is
unreasonable to attempt to coordinate an event which requires judging and
supervision in addition to participants for 24 hours straight.  In any case,
events which are for any reason flawed should be examined for possible
remedies before being rejected.  A naked ratling race (as it would focus on
the area around Freeport), setting an event forward into the future, or
perhaps a six to eight hour marathon would all be examples of solutions the
Events Division might come up with.<br />
<br />
Once an event idea has been tenatively scheduled and detailed, the plan must
be refined.  Checking the existing schedule of events for both your guild
and others is important.  Be sure to account for the schedules of those
guild members who are required to be at an event.  In addition, the exact
rules or conditions of an event should be carefully drawn up.  As a
hypothetical event (which is obviously not real, since we don't even know
how the game works yet), we can continue with the marathon from above:<br />
<br />
The Events Division does some research and finds that there are relatively
few events planned and two dozen guild members will be available to act as
overseers on a specific date.  At this point they clarify the rules.  For
every two contestants they need one judge, and thus limit the number of
contestants to 50 (a round number) for this event.  They decide that the
first 50 level one individuals to gather at the docks of Qeynos at 13:00 EST
on Saturday the 10th of January 2004 will be accepted, and that each one
must report to Guyner the guild Events Officer, who will act as head
official for the event.  At 13:30 the event will begin, and the judges will
follow the contestants wherever they may wander for the next six hours.  At
19:30 EST all contestants wishing to be judged for the end prizes may again
gather at the Qeynos docks, where Guyner will award prizes for the top three
places.  Before prizes are awarded, the judges will turn in the names of any
individuals who were disqualified or penalized for breaking the rules of the
contest.  Penalties may be assessed by any judge and cost one level per
penalty.  Disqualification is the result of three penalties.  Illegal
actions include: accepting gear from another individual without paying
market price (twinking), accepting buffs or healing without reasonable
payment (powerlevelling), grouping with a disproportionately powerful group
(powerlevelling), using bugs of any kind (exploiting), or any other offense
(which should be warned before being penalized) deemed cheating by Guyner.
Prizes will be awarded to either the character who wins, or another
individual designated by that player.  First Prize will consist of 100PP,
second is 30PP and third is 10PP.  In case of a tie, the prize will be paid
out to a maximum of three people before being divided.  Thus if four people
win second prize, 90pp is split between them, resulting in 22PP 5GP each.
This means that the maximum cost to the guild will be 420PP should there be
3 or more people tied for each of the places.<br />
<br />
This event example was not entirely fleshed out, nor were all things
possible accounted for.  In the interest of keeping this article to a
readable length I'll spare you any more details.  However from the above you
can see how just one event can take a lot of coordination and thought in
order to pull off successfully.  However, if you do make sure to plan events
well, you'll have more success and fun in the long run.<br />
<br />
Since I've run on long enough on this topic, this article shall have to end.
Make sure to take a look at the other August articles as they arrive.  Some
of them will contain information which relates to this piece.  Next month
we'll be looking more at events, though I haven't decided which aspect to
focus on yet.<br />
<br />
</blockquote>


